<article class="env-lemma" id="0ER7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0ER7">Lemma <span data-tag="0ER7">82.22.7</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/0EDT" data-tag="0EDT">82.2.1</a> let $X/B$ be good. Let $(\mathcal{L}, s, i : D \to X)$ be as in Definition <a href="/tag/0ER1" data-tag="0ER1">82.22.1</a>. Let $Z \subset X$ be a closed subscheme such that $\dim _\delta (Z) \leq k + 1$ and such that $D \cap Z$ is an effective Cartier divisor on $Z$. Then $i^*([Z]_{k + 1}) = [D \cap Z]_ k$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The assumption means that $s|_ Z$ is a regular section of $\mathcal{L}|_ Z$. Thus $D \cap Z = Z(s)$ and we get 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  [D \cap Z]_ k = \sum n_ i [Z(s_ i)]_ k  \]
</div>
    <p>
       as cycles where $s_ i = s|_{Z_ i}$, the $Z_ i$ are the irreducible components of $\delta $-dimension $k + 1$, and $[Z]_{k + 1} = \sum n_ i[Z_ i]$. See Lemma <a href="/tag/0EQL" data-tag="0EQL">82.18.3</a>. We have $D \cap Z_ i = Z(s_ i)$. Comparing with the definition of the Gysin map we conclude. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>